e] [17592186410573 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410569 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410573 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410570 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410573 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410573 1077 "selection" 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410573 1078 #edb/ref 17592186410564 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410573 1079 #uuid "bd4eb193-01d2-584e-8123-beed2a93cb45" 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410574 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410563 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410574 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410565 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410574 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410566 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410574 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410571 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410574 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410572 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410574 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410574 1077 "selection" 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410574 1078 #edb/ref 17592186410565 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410574 1079 #uuid "e9b7d5ac-eef6-5e03-9395-4cad5505dd2d" 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410575 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410573 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410575 1069 "q-104574" 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410575 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410575 1071 "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F=\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}\\,i-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{y}}\\,j$\n- $G=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y\\,i-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j$\n- $H=(8x-3y)\\,i+(4y-3x+1)\\,j$" 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410575 1072 #uuid "6b4c4358-44ac-586a-b065-3dc8b8db940a" 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410575 1074 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}$ and $Q=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{y}}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y$ and $Q=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}+1}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=8x-3y$ and $Q=4y-3x+1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-3.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410574 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410576 1069 "q-86896" 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410576 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410576 1071 "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F=({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{2})\\,i+({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{7})\\,j$\n- $G=({y}^{3}-6x)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2})\\,j$\n- $H=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,j$" 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410576 1072 #uuid "953d7cc2-ab7b-52e3-b63e-6dc18089f06f" 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410576 1074 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P={e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{2}$ and $Q={e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{7}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P={y}^{3}-6x$ and $Q=3x{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and $Q=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410577 1062 #uuid "a79f93a0-28bb-5df3-afcd-772f79c2b956" 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410577 1063 #edb/ref 17592186410575 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410578 1058 #edb/ref 17592186410577 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410578 1062 #uuid "b4f0c749-c70e-5cd0-8997-a35907ffaa6a" 13194139533598 17592186045417 true] [17592186410578 1063 #edb/r